                    </article>
                    <article style="border:1px solid #68838d;padding:10px;border-radius:7px">
                        <strong>New B — 15</strong>
                        <div role="img" aria-label="Original Edulytics model B: three complete rows plus a missing dot, so one column is incomplete."
                             style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(4,15px);gap:6px;margin-top:9px">
                            @for(var slot=0;slot<16;slot++){
                                <span aria-hidden="true" style="width:13px;height:13px;border-radius:50%;background:@(bPositions.Contains(slot)?"#a7508f":"transparent")"></span>
                            }
                        </div>
                    </article>
                    <article style="border:1px solid #68838d;padding:10px;border-radius:7px">
                        <strong>New C — 15</strong>
                        <div role="img" aria-label="Original Edulytics model C: five scattered mini-clusters of three dots each, not a rectangular array."
                             style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:9px;margin-top:9px">
                            @for(var group=0;group<5;group++){
                                <div aria-hidden="true" style="display:flex;gap:3px;padding:4px;transform:rotate(@(group*13-26)deg)">
                                    @for(var dot=0;dot<3;dot++){
                                        <span style="width:10px;height:10px;border-radius:50%;background:#a7508f"></span>
                                    }
                                </div>
                            }
                        </div>
                    </article>
                    <article style="border:1px solid #68838d;padding:10px;border-radius:7px">
                        <strong>New D — 16</strong>
                        <div role="img" aria-label="Original Edulytics model D: complete four by four rectangular array with sixteen dots."
                             style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(4,15px);gap:6px;margin-top:9px">
                            @for(var dot=0;dot<16;dot++){
                                <span aria-hidden="true" style="width:13px;height:13px;border-radius:50%;background:#a7508f"></span>
                            }
                        </div>
                    </article>
                </div>
                <p>The official first-edition 2021 Student Task Statements PDF has
                    three pages and source Activity 2 asks for drawing and explaining
                    both twelve- and sixteen-dot arrays. Teacher reviews card sorting,
                    student drawings and oral peer revisions.</p>
                <h2>Source Activity 2: draw, rather than just count</h2>
                <p>Reorganize 4 groups of 3 into a rectangular array; use 12 dots. Then use all 16 dots of the other source figure in two different arrays. Here is an independently drawn EXAMPLE of three rows of four dots; it does not replace the original source card artwork.</p>
                <div class="first-party-array" style="grid-template-columns:repeat(4,17px)" role="img" aria-label="Independent example: three complete rows, four dots per row">
                    @for(var i=0;i<12;i++){<span class="first-party-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>}
                </div>

                <h3>Independent visual starters for source Activity 2</h3>
                <p>Four independent clusters of three dots supply twelve objects.
                    Rearrange all twelve into one complete array, then explain where
                    the groups of three appear in rows or columns.</p>
                <div role="img" aria-label="Four small separate clusters, with three dots per cluster and twelve total."
                     style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,minmax(75px,95px));gap:14px;margin:10px 0">
                    @for(var group=0;group<4;group++){
                        <div aria-hidden="true" style="display:flex;gap:4px;padding:10px;border:1px dashed #5a7280;border-radius:12px">
                            @for(var dot=0;dot<3;dot++){
                                <span style="width:13px;height:13px;border-radius:50%;background:#b7602e"></span>
                            }
                        </div>
                    }
                </div>
                <p>Another original Edulytics starter has sixteen scattered tokens.
                    It is deliberately <em>not</em> already an array. Students draw
                    multiple complete rectangular versions using exactly those
                    sixteen objects; examples are 4×4 and 2×8, but other valid
                    rectangular orientations must be teacher-checked.</p>
                <div role="img" aria-label="Sixteen irregularly placed first-party tokens, not originally in equal rectangular rows."
                     style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;max-width:245px;margin:10px 0;align-items:center">
                    @for(var dot=0;dot<16;dot++){
                        <span aria-hidden="true" style="display:block;width:12px;height:12px;border-radius:50%;margin-top:@((dot%4)*3)px;background:#b7602e"></span>
                    }
                </div>
                <p>Show why all rows and columns form valid equal-sized groupings. Teacher evaluates your own drawings and actual peer explanation revision.</p>
            </section>
        }
        @if(l.UnitNumber == 1 && l.LessonNumber == 18) {
            <section class="panel" aria-label="Source arrays, expressions and human arrays">
                <h2>Warm-up: a five-by-four triangle array</h2>
                <p>Observe five rows of four triangles (20 in total), and explain how equal-size rows or columns help you count. The publisher's actual figure has not been copied.</p>
                <p>This source-equivalent image is independently created by Edulytics:
                    five complete rows of four triangular counters, twenty in total.
                    The published first-edition 2021 triangle SVG was inspected for
                    the mathematical 5×4 geometry; it has not been copied.</p>
                <div role="img" aria-label="Five complete rows of four independently drawn triangles, twenty triangles total."
                     style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(4,30px);gap:10px;max-width:165px;margin:12px 0">
                    @for(var triangle=0;triangle<20;triangle++){
                        <span aria-hidden="true" style="display:block;width:25px;height:24px;background:#a56a37;clip-path:polygon(50% 0%,100% 100%,0% 100%)"></span>
