                    where the student can justify the corresponding row/column grouping.</p>

                <h2>Source Activity 1: draw a separate array and write a multiplication expression</h2>
                <p>The four original array contexts are three rows of five chairs, four rows of five cars, two rows of six eggs, and two lines of ten pupils. Use objects or diagrams for each; enter the ROW count × GROUP size in the appropriate individual source task. Entering the total alone cannot demonstrate writing the expression.</p>
                <p>Here is an independently authored three-row by five-column array illustration (not original publisher artwork):</p>
                <div class="first-party-array" style="grid-template-columns:repeat(5,17px)" role="img" aria-label="Three rows with five dots each">
                    @for(var i=0;i<15;i++){<span class="first-party-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>}
                </div>
                <h2>Source Activity 2: arrays from an expression</h2>
                <p>Work as a class to form a human 4 × 6 array (adjustable for actual class size), then individually draw arrays for 2 × 3, 5 × 2 and 4 × 4. Teacher checks whether each array actually consists of equal rows and columns, rather than merely a set of isolated dots.</p>
