                </div>
                <p>The original 2021 Image B groups COLUMNS vertically, not five horizontal rows. Both pictures contain two rows of five dots, but changing which dots are enclosed as equal groups changes the interpretation from 2 × 5 to 5 × 2.</p>
                <p>Show both complete equations, 2 × 5 = 10 and 5 × 2 = 10, with an explanation of which factor refers to each kind of group. Their totals match but the imagined situations may differ.</p>
                <h2>Activity 2: one array, two complete equations</h2>
                <p>Examine the original 3 × 6 array shown by the teacher and justify how 3 × 6 = 18 and 6 × 3 = 18 can BOTH describe it. Work with several partners and revise your explanation after their questions.</p>
                <div role="img" aria-label="Independent Edulytics three-row six-column dot array, total eighteen objects."
                     style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(6,17px);gap:6px;max-width:max-content;margin:12px 0">
                    @for(var dot=0;dot<18;dot++){<span class="first-party-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>}
                </div>
                <p>Section summary model, separately authored: three equal groups, each with five counters (15 total), alongside the expressions 3 × 5 and equations 3 × 5 = 15 and 5 × 3 = 15. These are independent explanatory symbols and diagrams, not copies of source figures.</p>
                <div role="img" aria-label="Three independent first-party groups with five countable dots each, fifteen counters in all."
