                </div>

                <p>The original lesson synthesis also compares 3 × 4 = 12 and 4 × 3 = 12 before writing 3 × 4 = 4 × 3. The inverse order of factors does not change the product.</p>
            </section>
        }
        @if(l.UnitNumber == 1 && l.LessonNumber == 21) {
            <section class="panel" aria-label="Original optional source modelling project with independent illustration">
                <h2>Optional source project: plan a game night</h2>
                <h3>Warm-up: Notice and Wonder, before revealing the context</h3>
                <p>Look at this independently authored model first, without assuming what the six squares and surrounding circles represent. Describe what repeats and ask what information would help you understand the arrangement.</p>
                <div role="img" aria-label="Six separate square centers arranged as three columns by two rows. Each square has one countable circle immediately above, below, left and right: four circles per square, twenty-four circles altogether."
                     style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,76px);column-gap:17px;row-gap:18px;margin:14px 0">
                    @for(var table=0;table<6;table++){
                        <div style="position:relative;width:56px;height:56px;margin:10px;border:2px solid #436274;background:#e2f3ef;box-sizing:border-box">
                            <span aria-hidden="true" style="position:absolute;width:12px;height:12px;top:-18px;left:19px;border-radius:50%;background:#266c86"></span>
                            <span aria-hidden="true" style="position:absolute;width:12px;height:12px;bottom:-18px;left:19px;border-radius:50%;background:#266c86"></span>
                            <span aria-hidden="true" style="position:absolute;width:12px;height:12px;left:-18px;top:19px;border-radius:50%;background:#266c86"></span>
                            <span aria-hidden="true" style="position:absolute;width:12px;height:12px;right:-18px;top:19px;border-radius:50%;background:#266c86"></span>
                        </div>
                    }
                </div>
                <details>
                    <summary>Teacher-controlled reveal of the original context</summary>
                    <p>The six squares represent tables, and the four circles around each table represent potentially available seats. What information would you need BEFORE designing a seating plan for game night? This is not the final plan for sixteen tables.</p>
                </details>
                <p>Teacher access support: clarify unknown vocabulary with MLR8 and allow groups that need reduced complexity to work initially with fewer game types. Every pupil still creates a separate A–D graph in Activity 2, comparing their personal guest counts with their own group-designed seating plan.</p>
                <p>Original constraints: 16 identical SQUARE tables, one person per available side, and four games: A needs 2 players, B 4, C 5, D 10. Decide which games run concurrently and how joined tables share edges. No single room layout is required.</p>
                <p>Here is ONE independently constructed valid example for discussion, not an original publisher solution: run two games of EACH type. Games A and B use one table apiece per game, C uses two side-by-side tables per game, and D uses four tables in a straight row per game. This uses 2 + 2 + 4 + 8 = 16 tables, serving 4 + 8 + 10 + 20 = 42 guests.</p>
                <table class="source-chart">
                    <caption>First-party illustrative example — NOT the mandatory answer</caption>
                    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Game</th><th scope="col">Players per game</th><th scope="col">Games</th><th scope="col">Tables per game</th><th scope="col">Guests</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>A</td><td>2</td><td>2</td><td>1</td><td>4</td></tr>
