                </table>
                <p>A straight row of <em>n</em> identical joined square tables has 2n + 2 exposed sides, not 4n: two sides per shared edge disappear. That allows six players around two joined tables, and ten around four in a row. An A table may have two deliberately unused seats.</p>
                <h2>Source Activity 2: each learner draws a scaled bar graph</h2>
                <p>For this illustrative example only, draw bars with heights A=4, B=8, C=10, D=20. Choose a scale counting by 2 rather than 1. Your ACTUAL own plan may have very different valid counts and require a different suitable scale. Every bar's height must match the number of playing guests in your own seating poster.</p>
                <p>Record your distinct decisions, seating map, labelled axes and reasoned trade-offs in the source task fields. The classroom teacher judges your physical layout, game compatibility, graph precision and assumptions; neither the illustration nor a typed numerical result is an automatic pass.</p>
            </section>
