                            <div style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;max-width:245px;gap:5px">
                                @for(var group=0;group<model.groups;group++){
                                    <div style="@($"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat({(model.each==4?2:3)},12px);gap:2px;border:1px dashed #6f8295;padding:4px;margin-top:5px")" aria-hidden="true">
                                        @for(var dot=0;dot<model.each;dot++){<span class="first-party-dot" style="width:10px;height:10px" aria-hidden="true"></span>}
                                    </div>
                                }
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    }
                </div>
                <h2>Activity 1 — match source expressions to first-party rectangle cards A–G</h2>
                <p>Seven original source cards have been inspected individually. These Edulytics rectangles are newly constructed from unit-square cells and preserve row/column counts; original publisher artwork is not used. Choose the card LETTER for each expression and explain the side-length structure. The letter feedback does not certify the explanation.</p>
                <div style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:18px;margin:12px 0" aria-label="Seven original first-party rectangle cards">
                    @foreach(var card in new[]{(letter:"A",rows:2,cols:6),(letter:"B",rows:5,cols:7),(letter:"C",rows:8,cols:2),(letter:"D",rows:3,cols:3),(letter:"E",rows:7,cols:10),(letter:"F",rows:9,cols:5),(letter:"G",rows:8,cols:4)}){
                        <div style="padding:9px;max-width:max-content">
                            <strong>Card @card.letter</strong>
                            <div role="img" aria-label="@($"First-party card {card.letter}, {card.rows} rows by {card.cols} columns of square units, area {card.rows*card.cols}")"
                                 style="@($"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat({card.cols},12px);gap:1px;margin-top:5px")">
                                @for(var sq=0;sq<card.rows*card.cols;sq++){
                                    <span aria-hidden="true" style="display:block;width:12px;height:12px;box-sizing:border-box;border:1px solid #557b94;background:#daeff7"></span>
                                }
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    }
                </div>
                <p>The seven original 2021 expressions: 9 × 5, 8 × 2, 7 × 10, 3 × 3, 2 × 6, 8 × 4 and 5 × 7. Match each individually. Both card letter and geometric explanation matter.</p>
                <h2>Activity 2 — create five rectangles on your own unit-square grid</h2>
                <p>Individually DRAW, label side lengths and find areas for 3 × 4, 4 × 6, 6 × 3, 7 × 4 and 3 × 2. Teachers may use the publisher's reduced-task support of at least three of five for suitable learners. The five mathematical areas are 12, 24, 18, 28 and 6 square units; numeric feedback alone never certifies tiled drawings.</p>
                <p>Source synthesis compares the same 2 × 7 = 14 square-unit rectangle viewed as two rows of seven or seven columns of two. Rectangular area measures space fully covered by congruent tiles, unlike dot arrays that count objects. The publisher Cool-down is educator gated; Edulytics offers a separate first-party skill-equivalent task.</p>
            </section>
        }
        @if(l.UnitNumber == 2 && l.LessonNumber == 6) {
            <section class="panel" aria-label="Source area in square inches versus square centimetres">
                <h2>The same number of squares is not always the same physical area</h2>
                <p>All four original 2021 warm-up illustrations have been inspected: they show the SAME outer rectangular region (2:1 width to height) subdivided into 5×10 = 50 small square units, 3×6 = 18 larger square units, 2×4 = 8 still larger square units and finally 1×2 = 2 very large square units. Each partition has DIFFERENT-SIZED physical unit squares. The numerical counts 50, 18, 8 and 2 cannot be compared as areas without naming unit size.</p>
                <div style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:14px;margin:12px 0" aria-label="Four independently drawn equivalent-size rectangles with different square-unit counts">
                    @foreach(var m in new[]{(letter:"A",rows:5,cols:10),(letter:"B",rows:3,cols:6),(letter:"C",rows:2,cols:4),(letter:"D",rows:1,cols:2)}){
                        <div><strong>@m.letter: @(m.rows*m.cols) differently-sized square units</strong>
                            <div role="img" aria-label="@($"First-party original-area-equivalent model {m.letter}: same 210 by 105 overall region, {m.rows} rows of {m.cols} square cells, total {m.rows*m.cols} cells")"
                                 style="@($"width:210px;height:105px;display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat({m.cols},1fr);grid-template-rows:repeat({m.rows},1fr);margin:8px 0")">
                                @for(var x=0;x<m.rows*m.cols;x++){
                                    <span aria-hidden="true" style="background:#d3eafa;border:1px solid #6c7f93;box-sizing:border-box"></span>
                                }
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    }
                </div>
                <h2>Activity 1: same count, DIFFERENT unit squares</h2>
                <p>Partner 1 draws rectangles on one-inch-square paper; Partner 2 draws on one-centimetre-square paper. For each of 2 × 5, 7 × 3 and 6 × 8, the counts are respectively 10, 21 and 48 square units; however the first partner's actual region is larger because a square INCH is bigger than a square CENTIMETRE.</p>
                <h2>Activity 2: measure the SAME original square twice</h2>
                <p>Estimate the area of the teacher's 2-inch-by-2-inch square, trace/cut the source figure, and overlay it on both grids. It covers four square inches, but its area in square centimetres is NOT exactly 25; teachers must review the measurement and estimate. Do not round it or require a fixed integer from the original student task without measurement.</p>
                <p>Important: the original first-edition Student Task Statements PDF (3 pages) and two separate 2021 physical measurement grid handouts (1-inch and 1-centimeter squares) have now been inspected. A pupil records two estimates FIRST, then physically traces or overlays the teacher-supplied source square and writes the observed inch² and cm² results plus BOTH multiplication expressions. The original printed drawing is not reproduced, and the source does not prescribe an EXACT whole-number cm² total.</p>
                <p>The separate Edulytics examples below now require writing the unit alongside the number. Entering only “21” is insufficient for a prompt expecting “21 cm²”, and “21 in²” is the WRONG unit.</p>
            </section>
        }
        @if(l.UnitNumber == 2 && l.LessonNumber == 7) {
            <section class="panel" aria-label="First-party source-aligned square-area units">
                <h2>How big is ONE square foot or ONE square meter?</h2>
                <p>Source 2021 warm-up: a pupil notices a large physical square outdoors. The original photograph is not copied here. Examine these independently drawn outline models instead; they are illustrative, not real-size prints. Each AREA unit is a square, not a single linear segment.</p>
                <div style="display:flex;align-items:flex-end;gap:35px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin:16px 0">
                    <div><strong>1 ft × 1 ft = 1 ft²</strong>
                        <div role="img" aria-label="Small first-party outline square with one-foot sides, representing one square foot"
                             style="width:55px;height:55px;background:#d9ecf5;border:3px solid #34779a;margin-top:8px"></div>
                    </div>
                    <div><strong>1 m × 1 m = 1 m²</strong>
                        <div role="img" aria-label="Larger first-party outline square with one-meter sides, representing one square meter"
                             style="width:181px;height:181px;background:#d6eee1;border:3px solid #2b8060;margin-top:8px"></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <p>The original first-edition 2021 teacher examples also display physically sized square inches and square centimeters from the previous lesson. Show all FOUR: cm², in², ft², m², and ask which suits smaller or larger covered regions.</p>
                <h2>Activity 1 — Teacher-reviewed reasons</h2>
                <p>Suggest real spaces best measured by square meters, then real spaces best measured by square feet. Explain what one unit square would physically cover. A correct number without a physical-size explanation is not sufficient.</p>
                <h2>Activity 2 — First-edition source contexts</h2>
                <p>For each original context, select an area unit AND give a reason: a baseball field, the cover of your reading book, your classroom floor, a sheet of paper, a tabletop, and a phone screen. Several choices can be defensible with suitable reasoning; a teacher must review your examples.</p>
                <p>Four additional original 2021 source comparisons have a specific quantity-and-unit answer: playing card ≈ 9 in²; student desk top ≈ 3 ft²; sticky note ≈ 55 cm²; classroom floor ≈ 55 m². Type the COMPLETE quantity including the SQUARED UNIT into the source checks below; a number alone must fail, and the teacher still reviews your justification.</p>
                <h2>Optional scavenger hunt — source-page discrepancy</h2>
                <p>The 2021 original online lesson HTML requests examples for cm², in², ft², and m². The actual 2021 five-page Student Task Statements PDF instead requests cm², in², ft², and SQUARE YARDS on its last page. This is a real discrepancy between source editions/formats. Work against the one your teacher assigned; write down which source version you used and justify your area-unit choices.</p>
            </section>
        }
        @if(l.UnitNumber == 2 && l.LessonNumber == 8) {
            <section class="panel" aria-label="Source-aligned original first-party models of missing rectangle squares">
                <h2>Warm-up — one more or one less per equal group</h2>
                <p>Original 2021 source figures: 4 groups of 5 = 20, then 4 groups of 6 = 24, then 4 groups of 4 = 16. The original second image has FOUR clusters of SIX dots, not 'five groups of six' as its erroneous alt text says. These are independently authored grouped models with the correct mathematics.</p>
                <div style="display:flex;gap:18px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin:12px 0">
                    @foreach(var model in new[]{(each:5,total:20),(each:6,total:24),(each:4,total:16)}){
                        <div><strong>4 × @model.each = @model.total</strong>
                            <div style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;max-width:205px;gap:8px;margin-top:7px"
                                 role="img" aria-label="@($"Four separate equal groups of {model.each} dots, total {model.total}")">
                                @for(var group=0;group<4;group++){
                                    <div aria-hidden="true" style="width:66px;display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;justify-content:center;gap:3px;border:1px dashed #426a83;padding:5px;border-radius:9px">
                                        @for(var dot=0;dot<model.each;dot++){<span class="first-party-dot" style="width:11px;height:11px" aria-hidden="true"></span>}
                                    </div>
                                }
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    }
                </div>
                <h2>Activity 1 — restore Elena's hidden tile grid</h2>
                <p>In the original math situation, the source art has TEN inch-square tiles across and NINE rows down. Every blank position is another unit square. The completed region therefore contains 9 × 10 = 90 square inches. This first-party demonstration uses a different palette and layout from the publisher's original diagram.</p>
                <div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(10,21px);grid-template-rows:repeat(9,21px);gap:0;width:max-content;border:1px solid #416b87;margin:12px 0"
                     role="img" aria-label="Independent nine-row ten-column rectangle with some initial cells colored and blank cells outlined. The entire rectangle holds ninety equal square-inch tiles.">
                    @for(var i=0;i<90;i++){
                        var rr=i/10;var cc=i%10;
                        var shown=rr==0 || cc==0 || (rr==1&&cc<4) || (rr==2&&cc<3);
                        <span aria-hidden="true" style="@(shown?"background:#8ccee6;":"background:#fff;")border:1px solid #628296;box-sizing:border-box"></span>
                    }
                </div>
                <p>The original real-world photograph represents Portuguese azulejo tiles; the original photos are references only, not reproduced here. Count the area of the whole rectangular surface instead of counting the few visible colored cells.</p>
                <h2>Activity 2 — metric area without visible squares</h2>
                <p>Original source rectangle 1 has eight one-meter intervals horizontally and four vertically: 8 m × 4 m = 32 m². Original rectangle 2 has FIVE internal ticks along the top, which means SIX intervals between the outer borders, and height 3 m: 6 m × 3 m = 18 m². These independent rectangles are proportioned according to the original row and column counts; the missing grid is imagined.</p>
                <div style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:35px;margin:14px 0">
                    @foreach(var model in new[]{(width:8,height:4),(width:6,height:3)}){
                        <div><strong>@model.width m × @model.height m = @(model.width*model.height) m²</strong>
                            <div style="@($"width:{model.width*23}px;height:{model.height*23}px;border:2px solid #477598;position:relative;margin:18px 5px 8px 0")"
                                 role="img" aria-label="@($"Independent outline showing a {model.width}-meter by {model.height}-meter rectangle, area {model.width*model.height} square meters, with top 1-meter divisions.")">
                                <div style="position:absolute;left:0;right:0;top:-8px;display:flex">
                                    @for(var k=0;k<model.width;k++){
                                        <span aria-hidden="true" style="flex:1;border-right:1px solid #477598;height:8px"></span>
                                    }
                                </div>
                            </div>
                            <p>Each top space represents 1 meter; teacher checks student multiplication and unit.</p>
                        </div>
                    }
                </div>
                <p>MLR8 asks pupils to explain why an incomplete first row and left edge still determine the full rectangular area. Rulers or straightedges may be used to complete the grid. The original educator-gated Cool-down remains separate from our first-party skill-equivalent assessment.</p>
            </section>
        }
        @if(l.UnitNumber == 2 && l.LessonNumber == 9) {
            <section class="panel" aria-label="Source-aligned first-party ruler and tape rectangle lesson">
                <h2>Warm-up — five groups plus ONE more group</h2>
                <p>The original first-edition diagrams both contain 18 dots. There are five black groups of three plus a new group of three shown in a different color, first as separate equal groups and then arranged as a full rectangular array. These are independently drawn examples and are NOT publisher images.</p>
                <div style="display:flex;gap:24px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin:14px 0">
                    <div><strong>5 × 3 + 3 = 18</strong>
                        <div role="img" aria-label="Independent six separately outlined groups of three dots, last group visually differentiated, total eighteen"
                             style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,53px);gap:5px;margin-top:9px">
                            @for(var g=0;g<6;g++){
                                <div style="display:flex;gap:3px;border:1px dashed #56839e;padding:7px">
                                    @for(var dot=0;dot<3;dot++){<span class="first-party-dot" style="@((g==5)?"background:#bd5e30;width:11px;height:11px":"width:11px;height:11px")" aria-hidden="true"></span>}
                                </div>
                            }
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <div><strong>6 × 3 = 18</strong>
                        <div role="img" aria-label="First-party three-row six-column array, final column is a distinct group of three"
                             style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(6,15px);gap:5px;margin-top:9px">
                            @for(var i=0;i<18;i++){<span class="first-party-dot" style="@((i%6==5)?"background:#bd5e30;width:12px;height:12px":"width:12px;height:12px")" aria-hidden="true"></span>}
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <h2>Activity 1 — measure source rectangles A, B and C</h2>
                <p>Source first-edition 2021 A is a wide <strong>6 by 3 cm</strong> rectangle; B is a taller <strong>4 by 5 cm</strong> rectangle; C is a long <strong>10 by 3 cm</strong> rectangle in the original vector artwork. Those nominal centimeter dimensions were independently checked from the source SVG vector paths (28.3465 pt per cm), NOT inferred from alt text. Thus A = 18 cm², B = 20 cm² and C = 30 cm², so C has the largest area. Have students predict before using their real centimeter rulers to measure the original correctly printed 2021 handout. Physical print scaling is a separate teacher check.</p>
                <p>These drawings are independently authored Edulytics models; they preserve the relevant 6:3, 4:5 and 10:3 mathematical proportions without copying source vector pixels. On-screen CSS pixels must NOT be used as physical centimeter ruler markings.</p>
                <div style="display:flex;align-items:flex-end;gap:24px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin:14px 0">
                    <div><strong>A — 6 × 3 = 18 cm²</strong><div role="img" aria-label="Independent model A, six equal units wide and three tall, area 18 square centimeters" style="width:144px;height:72px;border:2px solid #397c98;background:#e3f1f5;box-sizing:border-box;margin-top:8px"></div></div>
                    <div><strong>B — 4 × 5 = 20 cm²</strong><div role="img" aria-label="Independent model B, four equal units wide and five tall, area 20 square centimeters" style="width:96px;height:120px;border:2px solid #397c98;background:#e3f1f5;box-sizing:border-box;margin-top:8px"></div></div>
                    <div><strong>C — 10 × 3 = 30 cm²</strong><div role="img" aria-label="Independent model C, ten equal units wide and three tall, area 30 square centimeters" style="width:240px;height:72px;border:2px solid #397c98;background:#e3f1f5;box-sizing:border-box;margin-top:8px"></div></div>
                </div>
                <p>Teacher checks source PDF p2 A, B and C against a correctly printed 100% scale sheet, zero alignment, the measured side lengths and the <strong>square centimeter</strong> unit. The source image of a ruler displays each centimeter subdivided into smaller tick marks; centimeters are intervals between whole-number marks, not the number of ticks.</p>
                <h2>Activity 2 — build with real tape and feet</h2>
                <p>Your team of four receives ONE area target from the 2021 source: 4 ft², 6 ft², 9 ft², 10 ft², 12 ft², or 16 ft². Use a real yardstick or ruler and masking/painter's tape to construct a rectangle on the floor. Label actual foot side lengths, check length × width, and have every student explain the layout. Only the teacher can assess the physical build. Never accept a typed number as proof the rectangle was constructed.</p>
                <p>First-party example ONLY, not a required solution: one 12 ft² target can be built as 3 ft by 4 ft. Other factor pairs or orientations may also be correct when physically feasible. Optional stand-alone digital practice has explicitly GIVEN side lengths (such as 7 cm × 4 cm) and is not a numerical substitute for source tasks A/B/C.</p>
                <p>Lesson synthesis: compare complete square grids, partial grids and invisible grids; measuring the two side lengths still determines rectangle area when the scale and squared units agree.</p>
            </section>
        }
        @if(l.UnitNumber == 2 && l.LessonNumber == 10) {
            <section class="panel" aria-label="Community garden area models">
                <h2>Paint a wall — choose and explain</h2>
                <svg viewBox="0 0 300 170" width="300" style="max-width:100%;height:auto" role="img"
                     aria-label="Rectangle six meters wide and three meters high">
                    <title>Wall with given side lengths</title>
                    <rect x="28" y="20" width="216" height="108" fill="#e3f1f5" stroke="#397c98" stroke-width="2" />
                    <text x="136" y="152" text-anchor="middle">6 m</text>
                    <text x="252" y="78">3 m</text>
                </svg>
                <table class="source-chart">
                    <caption>Coverage of one container</caption>
                    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Size</th><th scope="col">Coverage</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><th scope="row">Small</th><td>3 m²</td></tr>
                        <tr><th scope="row">Medium</th><td>10 m²</td></tr>
                        <tr><th scope="row">Large</th><td>40 m²</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <p>Choose enough paint and explain why it works. Several purchases are possible.
                    Prices are not given. This question does not require the fewest containers.</p>
                <h2>Garden — first and second readings</h2>
                <p>Elena has a rectangular space in a community garden. Her plants need at least
                    20 ft². Her soil can fill at most 30 ft². One side of her space is 3 ft.</p>
                <ol><li>Read the situation and discuss what is happening.</li>
                    <li>Read it again. Identify the known quantities and their units.</li>
                    <li>Only then open the garden questions below for the third read.</li></ol>
                <p>Work in groups of three or four. Agree how everyone will contribute. Use tiles,
                    grid paper as a scale model, and tools to make a poster. A centimeter on the paper
                    can represent a foot in your model; it is not the same physical length.</p>
                <details>
                    <summary>After solving — compare area representations</summary>
                    <p>A full grid, side intervals, and two labelled sides can describe the same rectangle.</p>
                    <div role="img" aria-label="Independent three-row six-column array of eighteen equal square units"
                         style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(6,24px);width:max-content;max-width:100%;margin:12px 0">
                        @for(var cell=0;cell<18;cell++) {
                            <span aria-hidden="true" style="display:block;width:24px;height:24px;border:1px solid #397c98;box-sizing:border-box;background:#e3f1f5"></span>
                        }
                    </div>
                    <svg viewBox="0 0 220 120" width="220" style="max-width:100%;height:auto" role="img"
                         aria-label="Independent rectangle with six equal intervals on the top and three on the left">
                        <title>Six by three unit intervals without a full grid</title>
                        <rect x="25" y="20" width="144" height="72" fill="none" stroke="#397c98" stroke-width="2" />
                        @for(var mark=1;mark<6;mark++) {
                            <line x1="@(25+24*mark)" y1="20" x2="@(25+24*mark)" y2="29" stroke="#397c98" />
                        }
                        @for(var mark=1;mark<3;mark++) {
                            <line x1="25" y1="@(20+24*mark)" x2="34" y2="@(20+24*mark)" stroke="#397c98" />
                        }
                    </svg>
                    <p>A 6-by-3 rectangle covers 18 square units. A square inch has sides of 1 inch;
                        a square meter has sides of 1 meter. Select a unit suitable for the surface.
                        These diagrams are conceptual models; screen size is not a physical ruler.</p>
                    <div style="display:flex;gap:24px;flex-wrap:wrap">
                        @foreach(var unit in new[] { "in", "m" }) {
                            <svg viewBox="0 0 150 130" width="150" role="img" aria-label="@($"One square { (unit == "in" ? "inch" : "meter") }, each side one { (unit == "in" ? "inch" : "meter") }; conceptual diagram, not to scale")">
                                <title>@($"One {unit} by one {unit}")</title>
                                <rect x="25" y="15" width="80" height="80" fill="#e3f1f5" stroke="#397c98" stroke-width="2" />
                                <text x="65" y="115" text-anchor="middle">1 @unit</text>
                                <text x="112" y="58">1 @unit</text>
                            </svg>
                        }
                    </div>
                    <p>The two unit models are not drawn to a common physical scale. A real square
                        meter is much larger than a square inch.</p>
                </details>
            </section>
        }
        @if(showMinuteClock) {
            <section class="panel" aria-label="Edulytics original analogue clock reading">
                <h2>Read an original analogue clock</h2>
                <p>The time is in the afternoon. Read the hour and minute hands,
                    then enter the complete time. This diagram is independently
                    drawn for this lesson's skill and is not copied publisher artwork.</p>
                <svg viewBox="0 0 240 240" width="240" height="240"
                     role="img" aria-label="An analogue dial showing the hour hand a little past two and the minute hand one tick past the seven; count the minute marks accurately">
                    <title>Read the hour and minute hands</title>
                    <circle cx="120" cy="120" r="94" stroke="#244860"
                            stroke-width="3" fill="#ffffff" />
                    @for(var tick=0;tick<60;tick++) {
                        var theta=tick*Math.PI/30.0;
                        var outer=87;
                        var inner=tick%5==0?75:82;
                        var x1=(120+inner*Math.Sin(theta)).ToString(System.Globalization.CultureInfo.InvariantCulture);
                        var y1=(120-inner*Math.Cos(theta)).ToString(System.Globalization.CultureInfo.InvariantCulture);
                        var x2=(120+outer*Math.Sin(theta)).ToString(System.Globalization.CultureInfo.InvariantCulture);
                        var y2=(120-outer*Math.Cos(theta)).ToString(System.Globalization.CultureInfo.InvariantCulture);
                        <line x1="@x1" y1="@y1" x2="@x2" y2="@y2"
                              stroke="#244860" stroke-width="@(tick%5==0?2.5:1)" />
                    }
                    <text x="120" y="48" text-anchor="middle" font-size="18">12</text>
                    <text x="192" y="126" text-anchor="middle" font-size="18">3</text>
                    <text x="120" y="197" text-anchor="middle" font-size="18">6</text>
                    <text x="48" y="126" text-anchor="middle" font-size="18">9</text>
                    <line x1="120" y1="120" x2="164" y2="111"
                          stroke="#22445b" stroke-width="7" stroke-linecap="round" />
                    <line x1="120" y1="120" x2="82" y2="173"
                          stroke="#167b91" stroke-width="4" stroke-linecap="round" />
                    <circle cx="120" cy="120" r="5" fill="#22445b" />
                </svg>
                <p>Use the small marks for individual minutes, not only the
                    5-minute numbers. The clock alone cannot tell a.m. from p.m.</p>
            </section>
        }
